###############################################################################
# 03 — Permissions, roles, role assignment and per-user overrides
#
# Everything that grants authority is behind step-up: complete an MFA
# verification (01-auth-and-mfa.http) within the step-up window — 600 seconds by
# default — before running the marked requests, or they answer 403
# STEP_UP_REQUIRED.
#
# NEVER COMMIT A REAL CREDENTIAL OR TOKEN TO THIS FILE.
###############################################################################

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8090
@accessToken = REPLACE_WITH_ACCESS_TOKEN

@userId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000001
@roleId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000003
@overrideId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000006
@projectId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000007
@roleVersion = 1
@idempotencyKey = REPLACE_WITH_A_FRESH_UUID_PER_CREATE


### The permission catalogue
# Correct response: 200 {items: [...]}. Not paginated — the catalogue is
# compiled into the binary, so it is a fixed list.
# Read `max_principal_type`: `ANY` marks the only codes an external CLIENT
# principal may ever hold, and today those are exactly the two client.portal.*
# codes. `is_dangerous` marks codes whose grant *and* exercise need step-up.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/permissions
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### List roles
# Correct response: 200, a page of role summaries.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/roles?limit=25&sort=code&direction=asc
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Create a role  — REQUIRES STEP-UP, idempotent
# A role is a reusable template and cannot name a specific object, so there is
# no way to express RESOURCE scope here: `RolePermissionInput` has no
# `resource_type` and no `resource_id` fields to put one in.
#
# Correct response: 201 with the role and its grants.
# 403 DELEGATION_DENIED if you ask for a permission at a scope you do not
# yourself hold — you cannot grant authority you do not have.
# 403 STEP_UP_REQUIRED if the second factor is stale.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/roles
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}

{
  "code": "field_manager",
  "name": "Field Manager",
  "description": "Runs projects inside their own department.",
  "allowed_principal_type": "INTERNAL",
  "permissions": [
    { "permission_code": "projects.read", "scope": "DEPARTMENT" },
    { "permission_code": "projects.update", "scope": "DEPARTMENT" },
    { "permission_code": "tasks.read", "scope": "ASSIGNED" }
  ]
}


### Read one role
# Correct response: 200 with the summary flattened together with `permissions`.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/roles/{{roleId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Update a role  — REQUIRES STEP-UP
# `code` and `allowed_principal_type` cannot be changed here: the code is
# referenced by operators and seed data, and the principal type is the envelope
# the permission set was authorised against. A different envelope means a
# different role.
#
# Omit `permissions` to leave the set alone. Sending an explicit `[]` is a real
# instruction to strip the role bare, and the two are distinguishable.
# Correct response: 200 with an incremented `version`. Stale version → 409.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/roles/{{roleId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "version": {{roleVersion}},
  "name": "Field Manager (revised)",
  "permissions": [
    { "permission_code": "projects.read", "scope": "DEPARTMENT" }
  ]
}


### Delete a role  — REQUIRES STEP-UP
# Correct response: 204.
# 409 ROLE_IN_USE while anybody still holds it — the API refuses rather than
# silently stripping its holders. A system role cannot be deleted at all.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/roles/{{roleId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}


### A user's role assignments
# Correct response: 200 {user_id, items: [...]}.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/roles
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Assign a role  — REQUIRES STEP-UP
# The subject comes from the path; the body carries only the role. There is no
# `granted_by` field — that is the server's to record, not the caller's.
# Correct response: 201, and the subject's `security_version` is bumped.
# 403 DELEGATION_DENIED when the role carries authority you do not hold.
# 409 PRINCIPAL_TYPE_MISMATCH when the role's envelope and the subject's differ.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/roles
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "role_id": "{{roleId}}"
}


### Revoke a role  — REQUIRES STEP-UP
# Correct response: 204, and `security_version` is bumped again.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/roles/{{roleId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}


### A user's effective permissions
# The union of role grants and overrides after the principal envelope has been
# applied. Correct response: 200 {user_id, principal_type, is_root, items}.
# Like /auth/me `capabilities`, this is a hint for user interfaces: the backend
# re-derives every decision per request regardless of what a client believes.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/permissions
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Grant one permission directly, scoped to one object — REQUIRES STEP-UP
# `resource_type` and `resource_id` are required with, and only with, RESOURCE
# scope. Prefer a short `expires_at`: a time-boxed override is easier to justify
# than a permanent one, and it cleans itself up.
# Correct response: 201 with the override.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/permission-overrides
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "permission_code": "projects.read",
  "effect": "ALLOW",
  "scope": "RESOURCE",
  "resource_type": "PROJECT",
  "resource_id": "{{projectId}}",
  "expires_at": "2027-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "reason": "Incident 42 — temporary read access for the on-call engineer"
}


### Deny one permission outright — REQUIRES STEP-UP
# A DENY override beats every ALLOW from any source. Use it to carve an
# exception out of a role rather than forking the role.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/permission-overrides
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "permission_code": "projects.clients.share",
  "effect": "DENY",
  "scope": "GLOBAL",
  "reason": "Contractor — must not cross the external trust boundary"
}


### Remove an override  — REQUIRES STEP-UP
# Correct response: 204. Removing a DENY restores whatever the role grants said;
# it does not grant anything on its own.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users/{{userId}}/permission-overrides/{{overrideId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
